        "subtitle": "Even a tiny health budget, if spent well, can make a difference",
        "text": "A  Getting medicine to the world's poorest people is a challenge. Hot, poor countries such as Tanzania have plenty of microbes but tiny health budgets. Dangerous myths stop many sick people in the countryside from looking for medical help. Even when they do look for help, it is often not available, because they do not have the money to pay for it and their government rarely has the money to provide it free. As they cannot afford proper health care, poor people are ill a lot of the time. And because they are ill so often, they find it hard to work the long hours of productive labour that might make them less poor.\n\nB  However, there is still hope. A recent experiment in Tanzania has shown that a small health budget can go a long way, as long as the money is spent carefully. With the help of a Canadian charity, the International Development Research Centre (IDRC), the Tanzanian health ministry set up a health project in two rural districts to the west of the capital, Dar es Salaam, with a combined population of about 700,000. Five years ago, yearly health spending there was about $8 a head. This figure included an estimate of the annual cost of trained staff and of the buildings used for health care. The IDRC added $2 a head to the pot, on condition that it was spent rationally. By this, the donors meant that the amount of money spent on fighting a particular disease should reflect the burden that the disease placed on the local population.\n\nC  This may sound obvious, but in this region nobody had any idea which diseases caused the most trouble, so the first job was to find out. Researchers were sent out on bicycles to carry out a door-to-door survey, asking typical households whether anyone had been ill or had died recently, and if so, with what symptoms. These raw figures were then analysed to produce a 'burden of disease' profile for the two districts. In other words, the researchers tried to measure how many years of life were being lost to each disease, including the harm done to families when the person who earned the money died.\n\nD  They then compared their results with the amount the local health authorities spent on each disease, and found that it had no connection at all with the harm each disease did to local people. Some diseases were badly neglected. Malaria, for example, accounted for 30% of the years of life lost but received only 5% of the health budget. A group of childhood problems, including pneumonia, diarrhoea, malnutrition and measles, made up 28% of the disease burden but received only 13% of the budget. Other conditions, meanwhile, received more than their fair share of money. Tuberculosis, which accounted for less than 4% of the years of life lost, received 22% of the budget. Vaccinations also seemed to be given too much weight, though the low level of vaccine-preventable disease was probably a result of successful vaccination.\n\nE  This small addition of money from the Canadians, an extra $2 a head, was enough to let the district health authorities make their spending reflect the disease burden, and it made the change to a more effective approach to health care easier. Health workers, mostly nurses or paramedics rather than doctors, were given a set of rules on how to treat common symptoms. For example, if a child arrives coughing, with a runny nose and a high temperature, the nurse is told to work through a checklist of other symptoms to decide whether it is just a cold or something worse. If the child is breathing more than 50 times a minute, for example, he is assumed to have pneumonia, so he is given an antibiotic and checked again after two days. In most cases, the cheapest treatments are offered first. Children with diarrhoea are given oral rehydration salts, which cost a few cents. If the salts do not work, the child is sent to a clinic for treatment.\n\nF  Drugs are now ordered according to what is needed; before, the government had sent the same package of pills to every area. Mountain villages with no malaria received as many malaria drugs as lowland villages full of mosquitoes, and areas where nobody had ever suffered from asthma received asthma pills. As well as this better distribution of drugs, people are now encouraged to use bed nets treated with insecticide to protect themselves from mosquitoes, and even the Masai, a very conservative tribe of nomadic cattle-herders, have started wrapping themselves in insecticide-soaked bed nets.\n\nG  The results of all this were remarkable. Infant mortality fell by 28% between 1999 and 2000, and the proportion of children dying before their fifth birthday dropped by 14%. In nearby districts, and in Tanzania as a whole, there is no evidence of a similar improvement over the same period, and anecdotal evidence suggests that better health has made the districts less poor. Could this success be repeated elsewhere? The government is keen for the lessons learned to be applied in other parts of the country — so keen, in fact, that it is pushing the organisers to move faster than they would like. Other countries could also copy the Tanzanian model, and donors should take note that, although more money is certainly needed to tackle poor countries' health problems, how it is spent matters more than how much is spent."
